-- title:
--   Properness of the two-chart integral model at q=3
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $q=3$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a nonunit of $A$. The statement is set inside a block of data for the reduction of the level structure at $q$: a finset $W$ of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\ q\ M'$ (rational affine geometric places at which the geometric $j$-invariant takes a supersingular value), an inclusion $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'\le \mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$, a `ConstantReduction` $R_0$ of $A$ on $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'$ with values in that function field whose residue map agrees with coefficientwise reduction of Laurent series, an index $\zeta$ for a primitive $q$-th root of unity, families $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ of valuation subrings of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$ indexed by $\mathbb P^1(\mathbb Z/q)$ and $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$ indexed by $W$, together with hypotheses describing the chart at $\infty$ by means of Laurent-series quotients, the transitivity, injectivity and permutation behaviour of $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ under the automorphisms $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\ q\ M'\ \zeta'\ \gamma$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, the contraction of each $O_{\mathrm{SS}}\,s$ to $A$, its compatibility with $R_0$ and with the chosen place $s$, its invariance under the level automorphisms, and the existence of a transcendental parameter on it; a `RegularProlongation` $R$ with $R.\mathrm{integers}=O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$ and the requirement that $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ be cut out by $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$; an element $\pi\in A$ with $\pi^{q^2-1}=q$; a subfield $k_0\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\pi_0\in k_0\cap A$ such that the contraction $A\cap k_0$ is a henselian discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ and algebraically closed residue field, and such that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0\cap A$; an auxiliary prime $\ell\ge 3$ with $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$, a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity $\zeta_0\in k_0$, and an element $\varpi_t\in k_0\cap A$ with $\varpi_t^{q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for a unit $u$ of $A$; finally a finite extension $K_1/k_0$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and the valuation subring $A_1$ of $K_1$ consisting of the elements lying in $A$, assumed to be a henselian discrete valuation ring. Give $\mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$ its $k_0$-algebra structure through $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. The conclusion asserts: for every intermediate field $F_0$ of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$ over $k_0$ such that $F_0$ together with the image of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ generates everything, $F_0$ is stable under all $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\ q\ M'\ \zeta'\ \gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, for every finite $K'/k_0$ the field $\mathrm{adjoin}\ k_0\,(\text{image of }K')\sqcup F_0$ is linearly disjoint from $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $K'$ in the sense that any relation $\sum_i c_i a_i=0$ with $c$ linearly independent over $K'$ and the $a_i$ in that field forces all $a_i=0$, and every element of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$ with rational Laurent coefficients lies in $F_0$: then, writing $E:=\mathrm{adjoin}\ k_0\,(\text{image of }K_1)\sqcup F_0$, for any $A_1$-algebra structure on $E$ compatible with the inclusion of $A_1$ into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and any $j_1\in E$ which, viewed in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$, is the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of the modular invariant and is nonzero, the morphism $\mathrm{toBase}$ from the two-chart integral model $\mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\ A_1\ E\ j_1$ (the pushout of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the two charts $A_1[j_1,\dots]$ and $A_1[j_1^{-1},\dots]$ along their common middle) to $\mathrm{Spec}\ A_1$ is proper.
--
--   This is the properness of the integral model of the level-$q^2M'$ modular curve over the discrete valuation ring $A\cap K_1$, obtained by gluing the two charts attached to $j_1$ and $j_1^{-1}$; it is the case $q=3$ of the corresponding statement for larger $q$, the Igusa leg of the covering requiring no further restriction at $q=3$. It supplies the properness needed to locate centred points, to move them by the level automorphisms, and to read off special closed branches on the model, and is cited by the three statements of that kind at $q=3$.
